  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "8f539d29",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "### Figure 2: Bivariate relationship or group comparison\n",
    "\n",
    "**Owner:** Echo\n",
    "\n",
    "**Question:** Which product categories earn the most revenue, and does the ranking change between revenue and units sold?\n",
    "\n",
    "**Observation unit and denominator:** Revenue is aggregated from 15,685 canonical order lines. Units sold is `sum(quantity)` across those lines: 20,154 purchased units, including repeat purchases and multiple units on one line. It is not a distinct count of products or order lines.\n",
    "\n",
    "**Tables and join keys:** `order_items` joined to `products` on `product_id`. Revenue is summed at line grain.\n",
    "\n",
    "**How to build it:**  \n",
    "1. Join `order_items` to `products` using `product_id`, then check that the join keeps all 15,685 distinct order lines. This is the base denominator.  \n",
    "2. Demonstrate the join-grain risk: join the item-level data to `orders` and show that summing repeated `order_total` gives an incorrect total. Compare it with the correct total calculated once per order.  \n",
    "3. For each category, calculate the two numerators being compared: sum `line_revenue` for gross revenue and sum `quantity` for units sold. If expressed as category shares, their denominators would be $16,467,385.48 total gross line revenue and 20,154 total units respectively. The chart displays category totals, so it does not actually divide by these denominators.  \n",
    "4. Rank the categories separately by revenue and by units sold; rank 1 is the largest value.  \n",
    "5. Place the two charts side by side in the same category order so changes in rank are visible.\n",
    "\n",
    "**Interpretation and limitation:** Yes, the category ranking changes substantially between the two measures. Laptop ranks 1st by gross line revenue ($2.75m) but only 9th by units sold (1,397), whereas Accessory ranks 1st by units sold (3,051) but 10th by revenue ($0.56m). Thus, high sales volume does not imply high revenue: category price differences strongly affect the revenue ranking. Here, units sold means the sum of `quantity` (20,154 units over 15,685 distinct order lines), so repeat purchases and quantities greater than one are counted; it is not a distinct-product count. As a grain warning, summing `order_total` after the item join inflates revenue 3.53x because an order repeats for each item. Finally, `line_revenue` is before discounts, tax, delivery and costs, so the figure compares gross line revenue, not profit."
   ]
  },
